UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 2, Lesson 1: Tints, tones and shades', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A tint is a colour lightened with white. A shade is a colour darkened with a small amount of black or a darker colour. A tone is a colour softened with grey or a little of its opposite. Value means how light or dark a colour is. Practise a value scale of one hue from pale tint to deep shade so you can choose the right strength for sky, midground and shadow.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nYou have blue paint, white, black and grey. How would you make a pale sky tint, a mid-blue sea and a dark shade under a boat?\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Sky: blue + white tint. Sea: mostly blue. Under the boat: blue + tiny black (or more dark blue) for shade.'' Small amounts of dark colour prevent muddy mixes.\n\nWATCH FOR\nAdding large amounts of black to darken a colour. Black quickly dulls mixes, so add tiny amounts or use a darker related colour first.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMake a five-step value scale for one colour. Then paint or colour a simple object using at least one tint, one mid value and one shade. Label each.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"A tint is a colour lightened with white. A shade is a colour darkened with a small amount of black or a darker colour. A tone is a colour softened with grey or a little of its opposite. Value means how light or dark a colour is. Practise a value scale of one hue from pale tint to deep shade so you can choose the right strength for sky, midground and shadow.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-VIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Make a five-step value scale for one colour. Then paint or colour a simple object using at least one tint, one mid value and one shade. Label each. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using tint on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Adding large amounts of black to darken a colour.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Sky: blue + white tint.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Adding large amounts of black to darken a colour. Black quickly dulls mixes, so add tiny amounts or use a darker related colour first. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating tint on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with tint, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about tint with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for tint. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for tint. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Adding large amounts of black to darken a colour.","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material.","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson.","''Sky: blue + white tint."],"question":"You have blue paint, white, black and grey. How would you make a pale sky tint, a mid-blue sea and a dark shade under a boat?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for tint; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of tone and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Adding large amounts of black to darken a colour.","A tint is a colour lightened with white.","''Sky: blue + white tint."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with tint?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named tone and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Adding large amounts of black to darken a colour.","Name tone and explain why it matters only, without linking it to tint.","Apply tint to new material and name the tone and explain why it matters used."],"question":"Which response best applies tint with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on tint. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Adding large amounts of black to darken a colour.","''Sky: blue + white tint.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Which response best uses elements, techniques and audience effect for tint?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for tint.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Adding large amounts of black to darken a colour."],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about tint?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Example: Ana makes five greens: pale tint with white, mid green, green mixed with a touch of grey (tone), dark green","Adding large amounts of black to darken a colour.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — A tint is a colour lightened with white. — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue.","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster."],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for tint?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name tone and explain why it matters only, with no linked example.","Make a five-step value scale for one colour.","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Adding large amounts of black to darken a colour."],"question":"Which independent response best applies tint to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about tint with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating tint on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using tint on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for tint. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with tint, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Adding large amounts of black to darken a colour.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Sky: blue + white tint.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y4-w02-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise tint.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Visual Arts","success_criteria":["I can use tint with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify tone and explain why it matters.","I can practise shade independently on a new prompt.","I can notice value needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A tint is a colour lightened with white.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A tint is a colour lightened with white.","A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about tint with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Ana makes five greens: pale tint with white, mid green, green mixed with a touch of grey (tone), dark green with more blue, and a shade with a tiny dab of black. She uses the tint for sunlit leaves and the shade for under the tree.\n\nTRY FIRST\nYou have blue paint, white, black and grey. How would you make a pale sky tint, a mid-blue sea and a dark shade under a boat?\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Sky: blue + white tint. Sea: mostly blue. Under the boat: blue + tiny black (or more dark blue) for shade.'' Small amounts of dark colour prevent muddy mixes.","explain":"A tint is a colour lightened with white. A shade is a colour darkened with a small amount of black or a darker colour. A tone is a colour softened with grey or a little of its opposite. Value means how light or dark a colour is. Practise a value scale of one hue from pale tint to deep shade so you can choose the right strength for sky, midground and shadow.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Adding large amounts of black to darken a colour. Black quickly dulls mixes, so add tiny amounts or use a darker related colour first. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Tint and shade.","caption":"Lighten or darken.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Tint</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">colour + white</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Shade</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">colour + dark</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Ana makes five greens: pale tint with white, mid green, green mixed with a touch of grey (tone), dark green wit Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Ana makes five greens: pale tint with white, mid green, green mixed with a touch of grey (tone), dark green wit Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about tint with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Ana makes five greens: pale tint with white, mid green, green mixed with a touch of grey (tone), dark green with more blue, and a shade with a tiny dab of black. She uses the tint for sunlit leaves and the shade for under the tree. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"A value scale guides choices.","caption":"Match value to place.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Value scale</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">five steps</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Use</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">sunlit vs shadow</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","options":["Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about tint with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAdding large amounts of black to darken a colour. Black quickly dulls mixes, so add tiny amounts or use a darker related colour first. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Ana makes five greens: pale tint with white, mid green, green mixed with a touch of grey (tone), dark green with more blue, and a shade with a tiny dab of black. She uses the tint for sunlit leaves and the shade for under the tree.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Sky: blue + white tint. Sea: mostly blue. Under the boat: blue + tiny black (or more dark blue) for shade.'' Small amounts of dark colour prevent muddy mixes.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Adding large amounts of black to darken a colour. Black quickly dulls mixes, so add tiny amounts or use a darker related colour first. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about tint with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA tint is a colour lightened with white. A shade is a colour darkened with a small amount of black or a darker colour. A tone is a colour softened with grey or a little of its opposite. Value means how light or dark a colour is. Practise a value scale of one hue from pale tint to deep shade so you can choose the right strength for sky, midground and shadow. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 2, Lesson 1: Tints, tones and shades","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Painting, colour and mood","week":2,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Ana makes five greens: pale tint with white, mid green, green mixed with a touch of grey (tone), dark green with more blue, and a shade with a tiny dab of black. She uses the tint for sunlit leaves and the shade for under the tree.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Tint and shade.","caption":"Lighten or darken.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Tint</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">colour + white</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Shade</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">colour + dark</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"A value scale guides choices.","caption":"Match value to place.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Value scale</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">five steps</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Use</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">sunlit vs shadow</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 4"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y4-w02-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
